62
00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:45.240
On excess returns, author Robert Hackstrom traces how investment risk moved from discounted cash flows to Markowitz correlations and sharps beta.

63
00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:51.000
A shift that made risk easier to scale, but not necessarily easier to understand.

64
00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:11.320
He didn't say anything about variability.

65
00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:15.640
He just said risk is if you buy above those discounted cash flows, you're taking on risk.

66
00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:19.240
If you buy something below those cash flows, you're taking less risk.

67
00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:21.880
And so, you know, he'd spelled it out in the preface.

68
00:06:21.880 --> 00:06:26.120
But you know, but but you know, uh, Markovitz didn't even cite that at all.

69
00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:30.120
He didn't cite what Ben Graham said that risk was a margin of safety question.

70
00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:32.680
It wasn't nobody was saying it was variance.

71
00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:37.720
He just decided that he was going to take it upon himself to say that risk was variance.

72
00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:53.560
And um, you know, somebody should have raised their hand and said, you know, I think you ought to think you might think about this differently, or just cite other sources, but the dissertation committee didn't force the hand, and and nobody said, you know, you should look at Graham or you should reread John Burr Williams.

73
00:06:53.720 --> 00:06:59.240
And you know, he got his PhD, and he acts and went to work for the Rand Corporation, and nobody cared.

74
00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:10.840
Uh the 60s came along, and um Bill Sharp, uh, who was at UCLA, he was a you know, never invested in the stock market, never owned a business, just like Markowitz, just a student.

75
00:07:10.840 --> 00:07:16.000
He was looking for a dissertation topic, and his professor said, Well, you know, why don't you go check out this guy, Markowitz?

76
00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:17.200
And he tracks him down.

77
00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:24.720
And uh, you know, the problem with Markowitz's, you know, work was that he was doing correlations between stocks.

78
00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:27.760
If you had a negative correlation, that that was a good diversifier.

79
00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:32.560
If you were positive correlated, then adding the stock didn't diversify your portfolio.

80
00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:38.560
And it was a real pain in the butt because even before Excel spreadsheets and things of that nature, you had to do that manually.

81
00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:44.720
And Sharp came along and said, you know, why don't we find a base factor of which to measure the stock volatility against?

82
00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:49.600
And he said, you know, the biggest indicate the biggest impact on stock prices at the market itself.

83
00:07:49.600 --> 00:08:04.480
And he came up with the beta factor, and he said, you know, things that are above beta 1.0 have more variance than the market, we'll call that risk, and things with less than 1.0 than the market variability, we'll call that low risk.

84
00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:06.240
The dividing line is simple.

85
00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:08.480
Price volatility measures movement.

86
00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:13.040
Buffett style risk asks what you paid versus what the business is worth.

